1. Introduction
The rapid development of online services and the Internet has been regrettably accompanied by growth in cyber-attacks, with phishing being one of the most common and effective attacks (Halgaš, Agrafiotis and Nurse, 2020). Phishing is a type of cyber-attack that uses social engineering and technical tricks to steal a user’s sensitive data (Khonji, Iraqi and Jones, 2013; Peng, Harris and Sawa, 2018a; Fang et al., 2019; Halgaš, Agrafiotis and Nurse, 2020). It is done by exploiting vulnerabilities found in system processes from an end user’s side; combating it depends on human knowledge and awareness, which can vary between users. Even if the system is secure enough

against cybercrime, an oblivious user can be duped by an attacker into revealing their personal identity data or financial account credentials. Even worse, it can put an organisation at risk by facilitating access to the organisation’s data. The global annual losses of cybercrimes calculated by McAfee are estimated to reach one trillion US dollars (McAfee, 2020). Moreover, phishing is a significant cybercrime that affects businesses and individuals (Sadique et al., 2020).
According to a published phishing report, 75% of organisations experienced a phishing attack in 2020, and 96% of the phishing attacks arrived by email (Phishing Statistics Report, 2021). Of all the security breaches, 22% were related to phishing, according to Verizon’s 2020 Data Breach Investigations Report (DBIR, 2020). Organisations have increasingly

