become targets for attackers attempting to steal money or, worse, their valuable data. The consequences of data theft are severe: it damages the organisation’s reputation and value. Moreover, it causes customer loss for sectors that hold sensitive data, such as the banking and telecom sectors. Therefore, businesses should adopt a robust phishing detection mechanism.
The most commonly used phishing technique is phishing emails (Gupta, Arachchilage and Psannis, 2018; Phishing Statistics Report, 2021), by which an attacker uses a fake email to trick the recipient into sending their sensitive information. There are many methods to persuade a recipient to reveal their sensitive information, such as clicking a link and visiting a spoofed site to enter their username and password, sending information directly, or downloading an email attachment that executes malware. The increasing number of phishing emails is alarming, according to the Anti-Phishing Working Group report; the average cost for attacks grew in the third quarter of 2020 for businesses from $48,000 to $75,000 (APWG, 2020). Moreover, the number of recorded phishing emails globally in the fourth quarter of 2020 increased by 18% over the second quarter of 2019 (APWG, 2020). Furthermore, in 2020, the number of malicious emails in Microsoft 365 reached 13 billion messages (Microsoft digital defense report, 2020).
Benenson et al. (Benenson, Gassmann and Landwirth, 2017) reported a phishing experiment that showed that 20% of the audience responded and clicked the emails’ specious link. When asked why they clicked the link, 34% of the respondents gave the reason that they were curious. They recommended that organisations protect employees, as much as possible, from the decision-making process of opening and responding to phishing emails. Consequently, the automation of phishing email detection using body email text is necessary to combat this growing risk.
Currently, we have various successful phishing email detectors, but businesses and individuals still suffer from phishing. The nature of phishing emails is dynamic because it depends on the human intelligence of an attacker. Over time, attackers further develop and change their phishing methods. Thus, we should confront this problem by finding a solution that matches human intelligence.
To deal with this problem, novel methods such as deep learning, which can compete with human intelligence, have been introduced, but these methods are still immature and need to be improved (Akanbi, Amiri and Fazeldehkordi, 2014). Furthermore, most of the proposed automated phishing email detection techniques to decrease human error use email metadata or attached URLs.
Previous works on phishing email detection have shown encouraging results, but more work on detection models is needed because the dynamic nature of phishing emails requires advanced methods. Again, few promising phishing detection models based on body text analysis have been proposed (Gupta, Arachchilage and Psannis, 2018), and more effort is needed. In addition, the graph convolutional network (GCN) algorithm has proved its efficiency in text classification (Yao, Mao and Luo., 2019), but no phishing email classifier based on body text analysis using a GCN has yet been proposed. This study is among the first, to the best of our knowledge, to investigate the effectiveness of using a GCN to detect

phishing in email body text to improve phishing detection accuracy and speed. Moreover, the proposed classifier can detect phishing from emails even if the email has no URL or attachments.
In this study, we used email body text to enhance phishing detection accuracy, as we believe that there is a significant amount of information under the email body’s text that is worth the effort. Consequently, we propose a new classifier for phishing email detection based on natural language processing (NLP), deep learning algorithms, and a GCN. The classifier determines whether an email is legitimate or a phishing attempt. To do this, the email body is analysed using NLP and deep learning algorithms. An evaluation of the accuracy of the classifier is then conducted using the metrics of accuracy, precision, and recall and compared with state-of-the-art models. On a balanced and labeled dataset, the classifier proposed in this research performs well. The accuracy exceeds 98%, and all evaluation metrics are superior to existing detection models.
The classifier investigation is only applied to English language emails and does not cover phishing websites or emails in other languages. This study does not include any analysis of email attachments or URLs. The proposed solution was implemented on the mail server to detect phishing emails. Nevertheless, it could have other applications in detecting phishing through text analysis in different common phishing mediums, such as social media messages, posts, or tweets.
The rest of this paper is organised as follows: Section 2 presents a research background that summarises some work on phishing email detection. Section 3 describes the proposed classifier model. Then, the classifier is tested and evaluated in Section 4. Finally, the conclusion is presented with an overview of the project’s future work.
2. Research background
Phishing email is one of the Internet’s fastest-growing cybercrimes. The goal of a phishing email is to steal the confidential information of a user by pretending to be a legitimate source. For cybercriminals, phishing has turned into a feasible business. Successful phishing attacks can cause financial loss for victims and put their personal information at risk. It affects even emerging technologies, including the Internet of Things and cloud services (Alieyan et al., 2019; Al-Qerem et al., 2020).
There are two main types of technical methods to detect phishing emails: blacklisting and machine learning. The blacklisting method compares the sender’s email address, IP address, or DNS address with a predefined list of phishing addresses, and if the data match, the email is rejected before it reaches the SMTP mail server (Khonji, Iraqi and Jones, 2013; Gupta, Arachchilage and Psannis, 2018). While the blacklisting method has a low false-positive rate, it primarily depends on the reporting of phishing emails from the recipient side (Fang et al., 2019). In the same vein, automatic whitelisting monitors incoming and outgoing emails and produces a collection of legitimate addresses. It also reviews records of past email communications stored within an email server for addresses to add to the list. Although whitelisting can be used to prevent phishing email, it is not effective enough to detect all phishing attacks (Jain and Gupta, 2019). It does not provide security

