Alternatively, machine learning can automate phishing email detection through various methods, such as deep learning detectors that automate phishing email detection. To improve phishing detection, researchers have proposed two methods to improve the classifier: 1) testing different algorithms, or 2) focusing on feature selection to improve the classifier (Toolan and Carthy, 2010).
Feature selection is defined as a method for obtaining a subgroup from an original feature group that matches specific feature selection measures. Through this process, the useful features of the dataset are identified. It helps reduce the dataprocessing size by removing unnecessary and irrelevant features. Good feature selection can increase machine learning accuracy (Jain and Gupta, 2019), simplify learning results, and decrease learning time (Cai et al., 2018). Three main feature classes can be used in phishing email detection, as described in (Aassal et al., 2020): Header feature class, which includes a timestamp, recipient server, content format, and other header information; body feature class, which includes two sub-classes (text features and URL classes); and attachment class.
Several studies prove the efficiency of using textual features of the text to enhance the performance of phishing classifiers (Jain and Gupta, 2019). Toolan and Carty (2010) studied 40 features used repeatedly in the literature. The features were categorised into four groups: body-based, URL-based, subjectbased, and script-based features. In their study, the information gain for each feature was calculated, and then the classifiers for each feature were generated and evaluated. The results of this study were favourable for body-based features.
Bergholz et al. (2008) addressed advanced features for phishing detection. The authors noted the statistical insignificance of improving detection by modifying the classification algorithm itself and concluded that using enhanced features improved email phishing classification statistically significantly. Based on an unsupervised algorithm, two groups of advanced features were developed to complement 27 commonly used features in phishing detection. The basic features included structural features, link features, element features, word list features, and spam features. The advanced features included the dynamic Markov chain model, latent topic model features, and word clusters of topics based on latent Dirichlet allocation.
Moreover, machine learning techniques are commonly used to solve classification problems and detect phishing using deep learning algorithms or other algorithms such as naive Bayes and support vector machines SVM (Kubat, 1992). Deep learning algorithms have produced impressive results with unstructured data such as email data (Halgaš, Agrafiotis and Nurse, 2020).
Deep learning is one of the machine learning classes that uses algorithms to explore and model associated data, where a computer model can learn to execute a classification job directly using neural networks and datasets that include texts, sounds, or images. In this study, the reason for choosing deep learning over other machine learning algorithms is the extensive work of feature engineering used to implement conventional machine learning algorithms (Nguyen, Nguyen and Nguyen, 2018a; Fang et al., 2019). Fur-

thermore, because the data in emails are unstructured, deep learning algorithms are the best choice for this work (Halgaš, Agrafiotis and Nurse, 2020). Moreover, using deep learning algorithms in phishing email detection proved its effectiveness, such as the result described in studies of (Deng, 2014; Nguyen, Nguyen and Nguyen, 2018a; Fang et al., 2019; Halgaš, Agrafiotis and Nurse, 2020).
A GCN is a deep learning algorithm described as a type of convolutional neural network (CNN) that operates directly on graphs using structured data through semi-supervised learning to classify nodes. The GCN algorithm is commonly used in classification problems such as document classification; it simply converts the document classification problem into a node classification problem. The idea behind the GCN, as explained by (Kipf and Welling, 2017; Yao, Mao and Luo., 2019), is to first construct the corpus graph by building a single large graph from an entire email corpus, so that email words and the email itself are represented as nodes. The graph is then fed into a convolutional network.
Moreover, using NLP on the email body text to classify email body features in phishing detection has proven to be successful, as shown in the studies of (Bergholz et al., 2008; Toolan and Carthy, 2010; Egozi and Verma, 2018; Peng, Harris and Sawa, 2018a; Fang et al., 2019). Deep learning outperformed several NLP tasks to achieve state-of-the-art results in tasks such as text classification (Glorot, Bordes and Bengio, 2011; Lai et al., 2015; Prasad and Reddy, 2019), machine translation, and information classification (Nguyen and Grishman, 2015a, 2015b, 2016). Furthermore, deep learning algorithms contribute to improving phishing detection systems based on NLP (Egozi and Verma, 2018; Nguyen, Nguyen and Nguyen, 2018a; Fang et al., 2019).
Lai et al. (2015) proposed a text classification model based on the recurrent convolutional neural network (RCNN.(They used a recurrent structure to capture the contextual information of the text. They also used a CNN to construct a representation of the text. They applied the model to four datasets and compared the accuracy to a CNN model, a recurrent neural network (RNN) model, and other conventional models. They found that the RCNN model outperformed all other models tested.
Yao et al. (2019), proposed a novel method for text classification based on graph neural networks. The main idea was to represent an entire corpus as a heterogeneous graph and learn word and document embeddings mutually using graph neural networks. They applied the model to four text-size datasets and compared the results with multiple state-of-the-art text classification and embedding methods. This model proved to be effective with a 97% accuracy rate.
Fang et al. (2019), produced the THEMIS classification model, which works on the email body and header. Instead of using feature extraction for text, the authors used deep learning. They used the word2vec tool to represent emails, and they extracted the char-level email header, word-level email header, char-level email body, and word-level email body. The RCNN deep learning algorithm was used to develop the model. The accuracy of the THEMIS model was promising at 99%, proving the efficacy of using NLP for email phishing detection.
Egozi and Verma (2018) proposed a classification model based on NLP and machine learning, with a focus on novel fea-

