tures. The model used 26 features, four of which were novel. These features were applied across 17 machine learning models, and they produced a true positive rate of 83% and a true negative rate of 96%.
Peng et al. (2018a) proposed a phishing detection model using NLP techniques. Their model tested the email’s body sentences to detect the probability of phishing based on four malicious rules: command or malicious question, insistent tone, generic greeting, and malicious URL. An email was directly marked as a phishing attempt if the sentence contained a malicious URL or if the email violated two malicious rules. To generate a malicious question blacklist, they used a machine learning algorithm. The precision was 95%, and the recall was 91%.
Nguyen et al. (2018a) proposed a deep learning model that focused on using NLP at the word level and sentence level separately, then combined the output with an attention mechanism. The model precision was 99%, and the recall was 99.2%.
Almomani et al. (2013) proposed a phishing dynamic evolving neural fuzzy framework, which adjusts the evolving connectionist system. It dynamically detected phishing emails, including unknown zero-day phishing emails, before it reached the recipient’s mail. Their framework was assumed to learn continuously with a minimal memory footprint and reduced the complexity with minimum rules for email classification.
Halgaš et al. (2020) proposed a classifier using an RNN to classify emails as legitimate or phishing based on the email’s text. The classifier proved its effectiveness and accuracy. Moreover, it could be used as a complement to the existing classifiers.
This study uses the NLP of email body features and deep learning algorithms using GCN to build an effective phishing email detection classifier to increase the probability of automatically detecting the possibility that an email is a phishing email.
3. The proposed phishing detection model
To build the proposed classifier, we go through three main phases. First, data from phishing and legitimate emails are collected and prepared for training and testing, as described in Section 3.1. The proposed detection model is constructed through deep learning GCN algorithms, as described in Section 3.3. Training data are fed to the classifier to construct the model and prepare it for evaluation. Then, the final stage is to test the proposed classifier in a supervised approach using the testing data to validate the model. A diagram describing the workflows of the research procedure is shown in Fig. 1.
Phishing email detection is a binary classification problem, where the classifier should distinguish between two kinds of Emails: phishing and legitimate. For a classification phishing task, the input is a collection of instances (emails). Each instance, also known as a record, is represented by a tuple (x, y), where x is the attribute set and Y is the class label or target attribute (Baxter et al., 2011). It is a supervised learning task as there is prior knowledge of the output targets set. The goal of a classification task is to predict the class of an unlabelled

sample. If we have a group of finite class C= {c1, c2,c3,.....} , an unlabelled sample x is mapped to one class in C. For the case of phishing email detection, the classification is a representation of an email with the label set {phish, legitimate}.
In greater detail, the classification process starts with feeding the classifier with emails that are processed to extract the body text. They are cleaned of noise and irrelevant information, tokenised, and prepared for graph construction. The GCN is trained to classify phishing and ham emails. The prosed detection classifier model used in this research is illustrated in Fig. 2, and the description of each step is provided in the following subsection.
3.1. Data pre-processing
The data need to be cleaned to exclude needless characters and words. After the data have been cleaned, formal feature extraction techniques can be used. Therefore, the preparation of email data involves:
