This study applied various pre-processing techniques to the emails, including email cleaning, stop- and rare-word filtering, and tokenization. Each is described as follows:
3.1.1. Email cleaning This process cleans emails of irrelevant information and nonEnglish characters. Nonalphanumeric characters and nonspecial characters, such as “?”, “!”, and “’”, are removed using Python Regex. White spaces are also removed. Fig. 3 shows the pseudocode used and an example.
3.1.2. Tokenization The tokenization process breaks each email into words based on white spaces. These words are referred to as tokens. In this study, we use a split() function. Fig. 4 shows an example of the tokenization process.
3.1.3. Stop-word and rare-word removal Stop words are very common words; they are usually words that help build ideas but do not carry any significance themselves, such as conjunctions, articles, prepositions, and so on. We used the stop words list from the Natural Language Toolkit (NLTK). The stop words list contains words like “off”, “no”, “aren”, “too”, “an”, “being”, “only”, “ll”, “o”, “its”, “them”, and “mightn”. Moreover, any word that appears less than seven times is removed. The pseudocode of the stop-word and rareword removal process is shown in Fig. 5.
