3.3. The GCN classifier
Each node in the graph takes the information of all node neighbours, including itself. Then, the calculated value is sent

to a neural network (Yao et al., 2019). Node vectors are scaled or normalised before passing the neighbours’ information to a specific node. Scaling is accomplished by multiplying A by its degree matrix D. The vectors are scaled because nodes with many neighbours have large vectors v, whereas nodes with few neighbours have small aggregate vectors, which can lead to vanishing gradients and prevent the neural network from further training. Thus, we scale these vectors to overcome the potential issues. A is multiplied by its D inverse two times to scale both rows and columns, and it contains the information of all nodes’ neighbours, including itself.
