In the GCN, when we use one convolutional network layer, the node can only capture information from its nearest neighbours. Therefore, the more convolutional layers are used, the more information from neighbours is integrated. Thus, the more information is needed about distant neighbours, the more layers we should have. The task of gathering information about each node from its neighbours proceeds in parallel for each node. With two layers, we repeat the informationgathering task of the nearest nodes only. Based on (Kipf and Welling, 2017; Yao, Mao and Luo., 2019), we typically need two to three layers in the graph.

