4.2. The GCN classifier setting
Neural networks algorithms have various parameters to set, such as number of GCN layers, maximum number of iterations, early stopping of window size and so on. Parameters are set to achieve maximum accuracy based on work of (Kipf and Welling, 2017) to be as (learning rate: 0.1; Solver: Adam; Shuffle = True; Early stopping: After 10 epochs; Number of GCN layers: 2 layers; Loss function: cross-entropy error).

4.3. Evaluation and results

To evaluate the effectiveness of the phishing classifier, the initial dataset, a prelabelled set of instances, was split into two disjoint sets: the training and testing sets (Hlavácˇ , 2016). First, the training set was used to allow the neural network to learn how to classify emails. Second, the testing set held the data to be fed to the neural networks without labelling.
Then, a neural network was used to predict the classes. The testing set was used to evaluate the performance of the neural network predictions by comparing the predictions to the manually labelled decisions. Following the evaluation metrics used in the literature of (Kipf and Welling, 2017; Gupta, Arachchilage and Psannis, 2018; Nguyen, Nguyen and Nguyen, 2018b; Peng, Harris and Sawa, 2018b; Yao, Mao and Luo., 2019; IC3, 2020), we used accuracy, precision, and recall to evaluate the model.
False positive (FP) is the number of legitimate (ham) emails misclassified as phishing, true negative (TN) is the number of legitimate (ham) emails classified as legitimate (ham), false negative (FN) is the number of phishing emails misclassified as legitimate (ham), and true positive (TP) is the number of phishing emails classified as phishing emails. The classification confusion matrix is shown in Table 1.
