There are two methods that are commonly used to evaluate a classifier’s effectiveness: the holdout method and the k-fold cross-validation method (Rithchie, 2018). In a holdout split, the classifier is evaluated by calculating the error rate on a new data set, which is the testing set rather than the training set. Practically, it is common to use two-thirds of the dataset for training and the rest of the data for testing. In the k-fold cross-validation method, the training set is randomly divided into k disjoint sets of equal size, where each part has roughly the same class distribution. The classifier is trained k times, each time with a different set held out as the testing set. The estimated error is the mean of these k errors.
For the evaluation, both methods were used. Hold out split depends on splitting the data into two datasets, one for training and the other for testing. We used 70% of data for training (6,005 rows) while 30% for testing (2574 rows). Moreover, we used 3-folds cross-validation method, by dividing the dataset into three groups. The classifier is trained three times, each time with a different set held out as a test set. The result of the 3-folds cross-validation presented in Table 2.
