The measures of recall, accuracy, precision, and f-measure are compared with other published studies to evaluate the effectiveness of our model. The results are presented in Table 5. It shows that the GCN classifier competes with other machine learning classifiers in phishing email detection with a high accuracy rate. It is good to note that Bergholz et al. (2008) who achieved higher accuracy, used machine learning techniques depending on feature extractions that required a domain expert to intervene in the process to reduce the complexity of the data and make patterns more visible to learning algorithms. However, our model eliminates the need for a domain expert and handcrafted feature extraction. Furthermore, they used eight link features that will not work with emails that do not contain links. In addition, Fang et al. (Fang et al., 2019) used the header and body to detect phishing emails, while our model uses only the body text, which reduces the memory complexity. Moreover, we have better precision and recall than (Nguyen, Nguyen and Nguyen, 2018a) and short learning and classification time.
In comparison to other deep learning methods that use images, this model is more efficient as involving the processing of images could lead to an increase in the run time and space as shown in the work of (Bergholz et al., 2010) who used modelbased features including images. They also found that even in emails with photos, the message text and information about links were more effective for phishing detection.
Theoretically, the memory requirement for the GCN algorithm is O(E) and the time complexity is O(LED + LND2 ). Where L is the number of layers, N is the total number of nodes and D is feature dimension and E is the number of the graph’s edges (Wu et al., 2021). Experiments have shown that running time of the model is on average of 3.89 minutes for training 6005 emails and Average of 1.08 sec for testing 2574 emails. which is considered short time in comparison

